{"id":"u-d016f766-2d15-43a7-a4eb-34479e1fb07e","src":"community","title":"Science and Development of Muscle Hypertrophy","author":"Josmar Saiefert","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/sGdFJp-science-and-development-of-muscle-hypertrophy","desc":"The central purpose is to provide an evidence-based approach to maximizing skeletal muscle hypertrophy\n. It aims to move beyond anecdotal evidence by synthesizing scientific literature, allowing practitioners to understand the underlying mechanisms of growth and precisely manipulate training variables—such as volume, frequency, load, and exercise selection—to create customized, optimal training routines\n\nNotes:\nMind-Muscle Connection: For arm exercises (Biceps/Triceps), prioritize an internal focus of attention (visualizing and squeezing the muscle) rather than just moving the weight. This has been shown to increase hypertrophy in the biceps by nearly double compared to an external focus\n.\nRest Intervals: Maintain at least 2 minutes of rest for heavy multi-joint exercises to preserve training volume (total weight lifted). Shorter rests (60-90s) are acceptable for isolation/machine exercises\n.\nRange of Motion (ROM): Perform all repetitions through a full range of motion. The stretched position of the muscle is particularly vital for triggering growth\n.\nDeloading: Every 3 to 4 weeks of high-intensity training, perform a deload week by reducing volume and/or intensity by 50% to allow for recovery and prevent overtraining","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":60,"joins":3,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-08-24T13:48:25.784Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Lower Body - Heavy","Upper Body - Heavy","Lower Body - Moderate","Upper Body - Moderate"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":3,"hi":5,"rpe":8,"x":4}]},{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":3,"hi":5,"rpe":8,"x":4}]},{"n":"Leg Press","sets":[{"lo":3,"hi":5,"rpe":8,"x":4}]},{"n":"Leg Curl",